{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 16, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a nobleman and a leader of the County of Tripoli a Crusader state in the Holy Land. He was born in 1140 and was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. *## Early Life* Raymond III was still a child when his father was assassinated in 1152 and he succeeded him as Count of Tripoli. His mother Hodierna acted as regent until he came of age. Raymond III was taken captive by Nur ad-Din Zangi in 1164 during the Battle of Harim and was held prisoner for nearly ten years. He was released in 1174 after a ransom was paid.\n\nDuring his reign Raymond III was known for his military campaigns against the Zengid and Ayyubid dynasties. *### Military Campaigns* He allied himself with the Principality of Antioch and the Kingdom of Jerusalem to counter the growing power of Saladin. In 1174 Raymond III supported the coronation of Baldwin IV as King of Jerusalem and became one of his closest advisors. He played a key role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and was a member of the regency council for Baldwin IV who was a minor and suffered from leprosy.\n\nRaymond III was also known for his diplomatic efforts. *## Relations with Saladin* He negotiated a truce with Saladin in 1180 which helped to maintain peace between the County of Tripoli and the Ayyubid dynasty. However the truce was short-lived and Raymond III was eventually forced to flee Tripoli after Saladin's forces besieged the city in 1187. Raymond III died in 1187 and was succeeded by his godson Raymond IV Count of Tripoli who was the son of Bohemond III Prince of Antioch. Raymond III's legacy was marked by his efforts to maintain the independence of the County of Tripoli and his role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was a skilled military leader and diplomat who played a significant role in the history of the Crusader